    contentEn: `### Tutorial: Color correction in DaVinci Resolve â€” Step by step

DaVinci Resolve is the industry standard for color correction and grading, and its free version includes the full color suite used by film studios.

**Step 1 â€” Create project and import:** Create a new project, go to Media, import clips, drag the clip to the timeline in Edit, then switch to the Color page.

**Step 2 â€” Read the scopes:** Waveform shows brightness (highlights near top 100, blacks near bottom 0). Vectorscope shows color â€” skin should fall on the skin line between red and yellow. Enable with View â†’ Show Scopes (Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+W).

**Step 3 â€” Balance blacks and whites first:** Set blacks to 0 and highlights near 100 using Lift (shadows), Gamma (midtones) and Gain (highlights) wheels. Neutralize color with the vectorscope targeting a neutral area; fine-tune skin with the color wheels.

**Step 4 â€” Match shots:** Select two clips, right-click â†’ Match Frame, then use Match Color to copy the balance from the reference shot.

**Step 5 â€” Style and LUTs:** After balancing, apply a LUT on the final node or save your own look as a still to reuse. Correct first, then stylize.

**Step 6 â€” Node separation:** Node 1 balance, node 2 skin correction, node 3 style/LUT, node 4 final contrast and saturation. Use parallel nodes (Alt+S) for selective adjustments.

**Step 7 â€” Export:** Go to Deliver, choose H.264/MP4 for social (Full HD or 4K), raise the bitrate (10-15 Mbps for 1080p) and render.

**Common mistakes:** Correcting by memory instead of scopes; saturating before balancing; applying LUTs to poorly exposed footage; burning highlights; forgetting to match shots.

**Checklist:** Scopes on, blacks at 0, controlled highlights, skin on the vectorscope skin line, matched shots, style after correction, test render verified on another device.

Updated August 2026.`,
