        contentEn: '<h1>Flexible dollar: more than $us 1.012 billion traded in banking in over a month</h1><p><em>The Central Bank of Bolivia reported that foreign currency operations grew since the new exchange-rate regime took effect.</em></p><p>More than $us 1,012,350,434 were traded in the Bolivian financial system in the first month of the flexible exchange rate, according to data released by the Central Bank of Bolivia (BCB). On June 26 the fixed rate of Bs 6.96 per dollar ended and since June 29 a flexible scheme applies, which began with a rate of Bs 9.73.</p><p>The negotiated amount includes dollar purchase and sale operations carried out in banks, exchange houses and authorized financial institutions. August 4 marked a record with $us 159,940,755 traded in a single day.</p><p><small>Source: El Mundo</small></p>',
