        titleEs: 'El tifÃ³n Dolphin golpea Okinawa y China cierra puertos ante su inminente llegada',
        titleEn: 'Typhoon Dolphin hits Japan\'s Okinawa, China shuts ports ahead of landfall',
        excerptEs: 'El tifÃ³n dejÃ³ seis heridos, suspendiÃ³ vuelos y cortÃ³ el suministro elÃ©ctrico a mÃ¡s de 50.000 edificios, mientras China cerrÃ³ puertos y suspendiÃ³ servicios de transporte.',
        excerptEn: 'The typhoon injured six people, disrupted flights and left more than 50,000 buildings without power, as China shut ports and suspended transport services.',
        contentEs: '<h1>El tifÃ³n Dolphin golpea Okinawa y China cierra puertos ante su inminente llegada</h1><p><em>El ciclÃ³n, que azotÃ³ la prefectura japonesa de Okinawa el sÃ¡bado, avanzaba hacia la costa oriental de China, donde las autoridades activaron alertas de segundo nivel mÃ¡s alto.</em></p><p>El tifÃ³n Dolphin azotÃ³ el sÃ¡bado la prefectura japonesa de Okinawa, dejando seis heridos, suspendiendo vuelos y cortando el suministro elÃ©ctrico a mÃ¡s de 50.000 edificios, mientras China cerraba puertos y suspendÃ­a servicios de transporte ante la llegada prevista del temporal a su costa oriental.</p><p>Cinco ancianos en Okinawa sufrieron heridas no mortales, incluidos tres que cayeron por los fuertes vientos, segÃºn las autoridades locales. Otra persona resultÃ³ herida en la prefectura de Kagoshima. Casi 39.000 edificios quedaron sin electricidad en Kagoshima y poco mÃ¡s de 12.000 en Okinawa.</p><p>Dolphin presentaba vientos mÃ¡ximos sostenidos de 162 kilÃ³metros por hora, con rÃ¡fagas de hasta 216. Las aerolÃ­neas ANA y Japan Airlines cancelaron vuelos hacia y desde Okinawa.</p><h2>Llegada a China entre domingo y lunes</h2><p>Las autoridades chinas esperan que Dolphin toque tierra entre la noche del domingo y el lunes y lo han clasificado como tifÃ³n de categorÃ­a naranja, el segundo nivel mÃ¡s alto del sistema de alertas. Elevaron la respuesta de emergencia al nivel III por riesgo de inundaciones y deslizamientos.</p><p>El Centro MeteorolÃ³gico Nacional de China prevÃ© que toque tierra entre Zhoushan, en la provincia de Zhejiang, y Fuding, en Fujian. Zhejiang, una de las mayores economÃ­as provinciales y un importante centro manufacturero y exportador, elevÃ³ su alerta costera al mÃ¡ximo y suspendiÃ³ las operaciones portuarias.</p><p>TambiÃ©n se cancelaron 78 vuelos en TaiwÃ¡n, la mayorÃ­a internacionales. El temporal ademÃ¡s tensÃ³ las relaciones a travÃ©s del estrecho de TaiwÃ¡n despuÃ©s de que China ordenara a los buques cumplir sus instrucciones de control de trÃ¡nsito, medida que TaipÃ©i calificÃ³ de "ridÃ­cula".</p><p><small>Con informaciÃ³n de: <a href="https://anewz.tv/world/world-news/23073/typhoon-dolphin-hits-japans-okinawa-china-shuts-ports-ahea/news">AnewZ</a></small></p>',
        contentEn: '<h1>Typhoon Dolphin hits Japan\'s Okinawa, China shuts ports ahead of landfall</h1><p><em>The cyclone, which lashed Japan\'s southern Okinawa prefecture on Saturday, was advancing toward China\'s east coast, where authorities activated the second-highest warnings.</em></p><p>Typhoon Dolphin lashed Japan\'s southern Okinawa prefecture on Saturday, injuring six people, disrupting flights and leaving more than 50,000 buildings without power, as China shut ports and suspended transport services ahead of the storm\'s expected arrival on its east coast.</p><p>Five elderly people in Okinawa suffered non-life-threatening injuries, including three who fell because of strong winds, local authorities said. Another person was injured in Kagoshima prefecture. Nearly 39,000 buildings were without power in Kagoshima and just over 12,000 in Okinawa.</p><p>Dolphin carried maximum sustained winds of 162 kilometres per hour, with gusts reaching 216. Carriers ANA and Japan Airlines cancelled flights to and from Okinawa.</p><p>Chinese authorities expect Dolphin to make landfall between late Sunday and early Monday and have classified it as an orange-category typhoon, the second-highest level. They raised the emergency response to Level III over flood and landslide concerns.</p><p>China\'s National Meteorological Center said the storm was most likely to land between Zhoushan in Zhejiang province and Fuding in Fujian. Zhejiang, one of China\'s largest provincial economies and a major manufacturing and export hub, raised its coastal alert to the highest level and suspended port operations.</p><p>In Taiwan, 78 flights, most international, were cancelled. The storm also fuelled tensions across the Taiwan Strait after China ordered vessels to comply with its traffic-control instructions, measures Taipei called "ridiculous."</p><p><small>Source: AnewZ</small></p>',
