        excerptEs: 'El presidente serbio, Aleksandar VuÄiÄ‡, reafirmÃ³ el apoyo a la independencia ucraniana y acordÃ³ estrechar la cooperaciÃ³n econÃ³mica, sin comprometerse a imponer sanciones a Rusia.',
        excerptEn: 'Serbian President Aleksandar VuÄiÄ‡ reaffirmed support for Ukraine\'s independence and agreed to deepen economic cooperation, without committing to sanctions on Russia.',
        contentEs: '<h1>Serbia respalda la integridad territorial de Ucrania en la visita de Zelensky a Belgrado</h1><p><em>Durante la primera visita del presidente ucraniano desde 2019, ambos paÃ­ses acordaron completar un tratado de libre comercio a fin de aÃ±o, aunque Belgrado mantiene su negativa a sancionar a MoscÃº.</em></p><p>Serbia continuarÃ¡ apoyando la independencia e integridad territorial de Ucrania al tiempo que busca una cooperaciÃ³n econÃ³mica mÃ¡s estrecha entre ambos paÃ­ses, afirmÃ³ el sÃ¡bado el presidente serbio, Aleksandar VuÄiÄ‡, sin comprometerse a imponer sanciones contra su aliado histÃ³rico, Rusia.</p><p>El presidente ucraniano, Volodimir Zelensky, concluyÃ³ una visita de dos dÃ­as a Belgrado, la primera desde que asumiÃ³ el cargo en 2019. Su esposa, Olena Zelenska, visitÃ³ la capital serbia en 2024.</p><h2>Equilibrio entre la UE y MoscÃº</h2><p>VuÄiÄ‡, que enfrenta posibles elecciones anticipadas en los prÃ³ximos meses, ha intentado equilibrar la aspiraciÃ³n de Serbia de unirse a la UniÃ³n Europea con sus vÃ­nculos histÃ³ricos y econÃ³micos con MoscÃº. Serbia condenÃ³ la invasiÃ³n rusa a gran escala de Ucrania en 2022, pero se ha negado a sancionar a Rusia y sigue siendo muy dependiente de su gas.</p><p>VuÄiÄ‡ dijo que Serbia continuarÃ¡ apoyando la integridad territorial de Ucrania, incluidos los territorios ocupados por Rusia desde 2014. Ucrania, por su parte, no ha reconocido la declaraciÃ³n de independencia de Kosovo de 2008, que Belgrado considera parte de Serbia.</p><h2>Acuerdo de libre comercio</h2><p>Ambos mandatarios acordaron fortalecer la cooperaciÃ³n econÃ³mica y completar un tratado de libre comercio a fin de aÃ±o, un paso importante para la candidatura de Serbia a la OMC y requisito para su ingreso a la UE. Ucrania bloqueaba el acuerdo desde 2005 por disputas sobre cuotas y aranceles.</p><p>Zelensky seÃ±alÃ³ que ambos lÃ­deres conversaron sobre proyectos de infraestructura conjuntos y cooperaciÃ³n en energÃ­a y seguridad alimentaria de cara al invierno. VuÄiÄ‡ aclarÃ³ que en las conversaciones no se abordÃ³ la cooperaciÃ³n militar.</p><p><small>Con informaciÃ³n de: <a href="https://anewz.tv/world/world-news/23079/leaders-of-serbia-and-ukraine-pledge-closer-economic-ties/news">AnewZ</a></small></p>',
        contentEn: '<h1>Serbia backs Ukraine\'s territorial integrity as Zelenskyy visits Belgrade</h1><p><em>During the first visit by the Ukrainian president since 2019, both countries agreed to complete a free trade deal by year\'s end, while Belgrade keeps refusing to sanction Moscow.</em></p><p>Serbia will continue to support Ukraine\'s independence and territorial integrity while seeking closer economic cooperation between the two countries, Serbian President Aleksandar VuÄiÄ‡ said on Saturday, stopping short of pledging sanctions against Belgrade\'s long-time ally Russia.</p><p>Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy concluded a two-day visit to Belgrade, his first since taking office in 2019. His wife, Olena Zelenska, visited the Serbian capital in 2024.</p><p>VuÄiÄ‡, who faces possible early elections in the coming months, has sought to balance Serbia\'s ambition to join the European Union with its historic and economic ties to Moscow. Serbia condemned Russia\'s full-scale invasion in 2022 but has refused to impose sanctions and remains heavily dependent on Russian gas.</p><p>VuÄiÄ‡ said Serbia would continue to support Ukraine\'s territorial integrity, including territories seized by Russia since 2014. Ukraine, for its part, has not recognised Kosovo\'s 2008 declaration of independence, which Belgrade considers part of Serbia.</p><p>Both leaders agreed to strengthen economic cooperation and complete a free trade agreement by the end of the year, an important step for Serbia\'s bid to join the WTO and a prerequisite for EU membership. Ukraine had blocked the deal since 2005 over quotas and tariff disputes.</p><p>Zelenskyy said the two discussed joint infrastructure projects and cooperation on energy and food security ahead of the winter. VuÄiÄ‡ said military cooperation was not discussed during the talks.</p><p><small>Source: AnewZ</small></p>',
