        titleEs: 'El NiÃ±o empujarÃ¡ a 49 millones de personas mÃ¡s al hambre aguda, advierte el PMA',
        titleEn: 'El NiÃ±o to push 49 million more people into acute hunger, WFP warns',
        excerptEs: 'El fenÃ³meno climÃ¡tico, que alcanzarÃ¡ su pico entre septiembre y diciembre de 2026, podrÃ­a llevar el total de personas con inseguridad alimentaria aguda de 225 a 274 millones.',
        excerptEn: 'The climate phenomenon, peaking between September and December 2026, could raise the number of acutely food-insecure people from 225 million to 274 million.',
        contentEs: '<h1>El NiÃ±o empujarÃ¡ a 49 millones de personas mÃ¡s al hambre aguda, advierte el PMA</h1><p><em>El fortalecimiento del fenÃ³meno climÃ¡tico amenaza la seguridad alimentaria de los mÃ¡s vulnerables; AmÃ©rica Central y Ãfrica austral figuran entre las regiones mÃ¡s golpeadas.</em></p><p>Cerca de 50 millones de personas mÃ¡s en comunidades altamente vulnerables del mundo sufrirÃ¡n hambre aguda por el fortalecimiento del fenÃ³meno El NiÃ±o, advirtiÃ³ el miÃ©rcoles el Programa Mundial de Alimentos de la ONU (PMA).</p><p>El fenÃ³meno, que alcanzarÃ¡ su pico entre septiembre y diciembre de 2026, podrÃ­a llevar el nÃºmero de personas con inseguridad alimentaria aguda de 225 a 274 millones en 45 paÃ­ses analizados, un aumento de aproximadamente el 22%. "El NiÃ±o es una amenaza masiva para la seguridad alimentaria de millones de personas que ya son vulnerables", dijo Carl Skau, director ejecutivo interino del PMA.</p><h2>Regiones mÃ¡s afectadas</h2><p>Se espera que partes de AmÃ©rica Central y Ãfrica austral soporten el mayor impacto. AmÃ©rica Central podrÃ­a registrar el mayor incremento proporcional (83,1%) de todas las Ã¡reas analizadas, mientras que Ãfrica oriental y austral podrÃ­an ver deteriorarse la seguridad alimentaria de mÃ¡s de 18 millones de personas.</p><p>En LatinoamÃ©rica y el Caribe, mÃ¡s de 16 millones de personas podrÃ­an verse afectadas, y Asia-PacÃ­fico sumarÃ­a 8,2 millones adicionales. Ãfrica occidental y central verÃ­a un aumento del 12%, con cerca de seis millones de personas mÃ¡s afectadas.</p><p>El PMA seÃ±alÃ³ que cada dÃ³lar invertido en prevenciÃ³n puede ahorrar entre 3 y 7 dÃ³lares en pÃ©rdidas y costos de respuesta. La agencia ha activado planes de acciÃ³n anticipatoria en mÃ¡s de 50 paÃ­ses y ya ha movilizado mÃ¡s de 14 millones de dÃ³lares en SudÃ¡n del Sur, Chad, Mauritania, Uganda, Guatemala y El Salvador.</p><p>Los efectos del El NiÃ±o de 2026-2027 se sentirÃ¡n a lo largo de 2027 por el impacto en futuras cosechas. La agencia advirtiÃ³ que las sequÃ­as, las inundaciones y las temperaturas extremas ya estÃ¡n poniendo en riesgo a las comunidades.</p><p><small>Con informaciÃ³n de: <a href="https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/08/1168079">UN News</a></small></p>',
        contentEn: '<h1>El NiÃ±o to push 49 million more people into acute hunger, WFP warns</h1><p><em>The strengthening climate phenomenon threatens food security for the most vulnerable; Central America and southern Africa are among the hardest-hit regions.</em></p><p>Nearly 50 million more people in highly vulnerable communities globally are set to be pushed into acute hunger because of the strengthening El NiÃ±o weather phenomenon, the UN World Food Programme (WFP) said on Wednesday.</p><p>The phenomenon, peaking between September and December 2026, could raise the number of acutely food-insecure people from 225 million to 274 million across 45 countries analysed, an increase of around 22 percent. "El NiÃ±o is a massive threat to the food security of millions who are already vulnerable," said Carl Skau, WFP\'s Acting Executive Director.</p><p>Parts of Central America and southern Africa are expected to bear the brunt. Central America is projected to have the largest proportional increase (83.1 percent) of all areas analysed, while more than 18 million people in East and Southern Africa could see their food security deteriorate.</p><p>In Latin America and the Caribbean, more than 16 million people could be affected, and Asia-Pacific would add an estimated 8.2 million more. West and Central Africa would see food insecurity increase by 12 percent, affecting nearly six million additional people.</p><p>WFP noted that every dollar invested in prevention can save between $3 and $7 in avoided losses and response costs. The agency has active anticipatory action plans in more than 50 countries and has already provided over $14 million in lifesaving responses in South Sudan, Chad, Mauritania, Uganda, Guatemala and El Salvador.</p><p>The effects of the 2026-2027 El NiÃ±o will be felt throughout 2027 due to impacts on future harvests. The agency warned that droughts, flooding and extreme temperatures are already putting communities at risk.</p><p><small>Source: UN News</small></p>',
