        slug: 'replicas-terremoto-colombia-probabilidad-nuevo-sismo',
        titleEs: 'Â¿CuÃ¡ntas rÃ©plicas han ocurrido y quÃ© probabilidad hay de otro gran sismo en Colombia?',
        titleEn: 'How many aftershocks have occurred and how likely is another big quake in Colombia?',
        excerptEs: 'El SGC reportÃ³ varias rÃ©plicas tras el sismo principal. Los sismÃ³logos explican que la probabilidad de un sismo mayor en la misma secuencia es de aproximadamente 5%.',
        excerptEn: 'The SGC reported several aftershocks after the main quake. Seismologists explain that the probability of a larger quake in the same sequence is about 5%.',
        contentEs: '<h1>Â¿CuÃ¡ntas rÃ©plicas han ocurrido y quÃ© probabilidad hay de otro gran sismo en Colombia?</h1><p><em>Tras el sismo principal de magnitud 7,4, el Servicio GeolÃ³gico Colombiano registrÃ³ varias rÃ©plicas, entre ellas una de magnitud 5,0 a las 8:18 a.m. en San JosÃ© del Palmar.</em></p><p>DespuÃ©s del terremoto de magnitud 7,4 registrado este lunes en San JosÃ© del Palmar, ChocÃ³, el Servicio GeolÃ³gico Colombiano (SGC) reportÃ³ varias rÃ©plicas en la zona del epicentro. La mÃ¡s fuerte, de magnitud 5,0, ocurriÃ³ a las 8:18 a.m., seguida por un sismo de magnitud 3,8 a las 10:01 a.m.</p><p>El SGC explicÃ³ que la actividad sÃ­smica estÃ¡ asociada a la "dinÃ¡mica tectÃ³nica natural del occidente colombiano", relacionada con la subducciÃ³n de la placa de Nazca bajo la placa Suramericana.</p><h2>Â¿QuÃ© probabilidad hay de otro sismo mayor?</h2><p>SegÃºn el Servicio GeolÃ³gico de Estados Unidos (USGS), a nivel mundial la probabilidad de que un terremoto sea seguido por otro mayor en un lapso de una semana es de aproximadamente el 5%. Es decir, en cerca del 95% de los casos el sismo inicial no es seguido por un evento mayor.</p><p>Sin embargo, las rÃ©plicas son parte normal de la secuencia posterior a un gran terremoto. El USGS seÃ±ala que la actividad de rÃ©plicas generalmente disminuye con el tiempo, aunque pueden continuar durante dÃ­as, semanas o incluso mÃ¡s.</p><h2>Recomendaciones</h2><p>Las autoridades recomiendan a la poblaciÃ³n no ingresar a estructuras visiblemente daÃ±adas y permanecer atentos a los reportes oficiales de los organismos de monitoreo sÃ­smico, ante la posibilidad de nuevas rÃ©plicas.</p><p><small>Con informaciÃ³n de: <a href="https://www.tropicanafm.com/2026/cuantas-replicas-van-tras-terremoto-hoy-10-de-agosto-en-colombia-ultimo-reporte-servicio-geologico-473050.html">Tropicana FM</a> y <a href="https://colombiaone.com/2026/08/10/earthquake-colombia-aftershocks/">ColombiaOne</a></small></p>',
        contentEn: '<h1>How many aftershocks have occurred and how likely is another big quake in Colombia?</h1><p><em>After the main magnitude 7.4 quake, the Colombian Geological Survey recorded several aftershocks, including a magnitude 5.0 at 8:18 a.m. in San JosÃ© del Palmar.</em></p><p>After the magnitude 7.4 earthquake recorded on Monday in San JosÃ© del Palmar, ChocÃ³, the Colombian Geological Survey (SGC) reported several aftershocks near the epicenter. The strongest, a magnitude 5.0, occurred at 8:18 a.m., followed by a magnitude 3.8 quake at 10:01 a.m.</p><p>The SGC explained that the seismic activity is linked to the "natural tectonic dynamics of western Colombia", related to the subduction of the Nazca plate beneath the South American plate.</p><h2>How likely is another larger quake?</h2><p>According to the United States Geological Survey (USGS), worldwide the probability that an earthquake will be followed by a larger one within a week is approximately 5%. That is, about 95% of the time the initial quake is not followed by a larger event.</p><p>However, aftershocks are a normal part of the sequence following a major earthquake. The USGS notes that aftershock activity generally decreases over time, although it can continue for days, weeks or even longer.</p><h2>Recommendations</h2><p>Authorities urge residents not to enter visibly damaged structures and to stay alert for official reports from seismic monitoring agencies, given the possibility of further aftershocks.</p><p><small>Source: Tropicana FM and ColombiaOne</small></p>',
