        slug: '3-frases-legales-para-un-policia-si-te-quiere-revisar',
        titleEs: '3 frases legales para decirle a un policÃ­a si te quiere revisar',
        titleEn: '3 legal phrases to tell a police officer who wants to search you',
        excerptEs: 'En MÃ©xico las revisiones de "rutina" no existen en la ley. Toda revisiÃ³n necesita un motivo y una sospecha razonable. Estas tres frases obligan a la autoridad a justificarse.',
        excerptEn: 'In Mexico, "routine" searches do not exist in the law. Every search needs a reason and reasonable suspicion. These three phrases force the authority to justify itself.',
        contentEs: `<h1>3 frases legales para decirle a un policÃ­a si te quiere revisar</h1><p><em>La revisiÃ³n de rutina no existe en MÃ©xico: toda detenciÃ³n o inspecciÃ³n necesita un motivo y una sospecha razonable. Estas tres frases te ayudan a exigir el fundamento con respeto y dejar constancia.</em></p><p>En MÃ©xico las revisiones de rutina no existen: no estÃ¡n en la ley, no aparecen en la ConstituciÃ³n y ningÃºn oficial puede detenerte o revisarte solo por caminar por la calle o conducir. SegÃºn la informaciÃ³n difundida por Badabun, toda revisiÃ³n necesita un <strong>motivo claro y una sospecha razonable</strong>, y tÃº tienes derecho a preguntarlo.</p><h2>Por quÃ© la revisiÃ³n de rutina es un mito</h2><p>El <strong>artÃ­culo 16 de la ConstituciÃ³n</strong> dice que nadie puede ser molestado en su persona, familia, papeles o posesiones sin una causa legal fundada y motivada. Para revisarte debe existir un indicio real de que cometiste un delito o de que llevas algo relacionado con uno. La palabra "rutina" no estÃ¡ en ningÃºn cÃ³digo. Hay una excepciÃ³n: si cometiste una infracciÃ³n de trÃ¡nsito, sÃ­ pueden pararte por eso, pero pararte por una infracciÃ³n no autoriza a revisar el interior del carro ni tus pertenencias.</p><h2>Frase uno: pregunta el motivo</h2><p><em>"Disculpe, oficial, Â¿cuÃ¡l es el motivo especÃ­fico de la detenciÃ³n o revisiÃ³n, y cuÃ¡l es la sospecha razonable que la justifica?"</em> Esta pregunta obliga a la autoridad a poner sobre la mesa la causa legal. Si no puede nombrarla, la revisiÃ³n queda sin sustento y puede invalidar procedimientos posteriores. Hazla en cuanto empiece la interacciÃ³n.</p><h2>Frase dos: pide la base legal</h2><p><em>"Con respeto, Â¿me podrÃ­a indicar la base legal que me obliga a mostrar ese documento u objeto personal en esta situaciÃ³n?"</em> Sirve especialmente para el celular, la mochila, la cartera o la cajuela. Tus objetos personales estÃ¡n protegidos y solo pueden inspeccionarse ante un indicio concreto de delito.</p><h2>Frase tres: pide identificaciÃ³n</h2><p><em>"Â¿SerÃ­a tan amable de darme su nombre y nÃºmero de placa, y confirmarme que estÃ¡ facultado para realizar este procedimiento?"</em> Toda autoridad estÃ¡ obligada a identificarse. Ese dato es el que despuÃ©s permite presentar una queja ante Asuntos Internos o una ComisiÃ³n de Derechos Humanos.</p><h2>QuÃ© hacer si aun asÃ­ te revisan</h2><p>No forcejees, no corras y no insultes. Grabar en la vÃ­a pÃºblica es legal. Avisa a alguien de confianza dÃ³nde estÃ¡s. Si te suben a una patrulla, guarda silencio, no firmes nada que no hayas leÃ­do y exige comunicarte con un abogado o la defensorÃ­a pÃºblica.</p><p>El tono importa mÃ¡s que las palabras: estas frases funcionan porque se dicen con calma. Se trata de que la autoridad entienda que conoces tus derechos y que la interacciÃ³n estÃ¡ siendo observada. Esta informaciÃ³n es de carÃ¡cter general y no sustituye la asesorÃ­a de un abogado.</p><p><small><strong>Nota editorial:</strong> El fundamento del artÃ­culo 16 constitucional es verificable en fuentes oficiales; la redacciÃ³n de las frases y los consejos provienen de la informaciÃ³n difundida por Badabun y son orientativos. Con informaciÃ³n de: <a href="https://badabun.com/mexico/3-frases-legales-que-debes-decirle-a-un-policia-si-te-quiere-revisar/">Badabun</a></small></p>`,
        contentEn: `<h1>3 legal phrases to tell a police officer who wants to search you</h1><p><em>The routine search does not exist in Mexico: every detention or inspection needs a reason and reasonable suspicion. These three phrases help you demand the legal basis with respect and leave a record.</em></p><p>In Mexico routine searches do not exist: they are not in the law, they are not in the Constitution and no officer can stop you or search you just for walking down the street or driving. According to information shared by Badabun, every search needs a <strong>clear reason and reasonable suspicion</strong>, and you have the right to ask.</p><h2>Why the routine search is a myth</h2><p><strong>Article 16 of the Constitution</strong> says that no one can be bothered in their person, family, papers or possessions without a legal cause that is grounded and motivated. To search you, there must be a real indication that you committed a crime or that you carry something related to one. The word "routine" is not in any code. There is an exception: if you committed a traffic violation, they can stop you for that, but stopping you for a violation does not authorize them to search inside your car or your belongings.</p><h2>Phrase one: ask the reason</h2><p><em>"Excuse me, officer, what is the specific reason for the detention or search, and what reasonable suspicion justifies it?"</em> This question forces the authority to put the legal cause on the table. If they cannot name it, the search lacks support and can invalidate later procedures. Ask it as soon as the interaction begins.</p><h2>Phrase two: ask for the legal basis</h2><p><em>"Respectfully, could you tell me the legal basis that requires me to show that document or personal item in this situation?"</em> It works especially for your phone, backpack, wallet or trunk. Your personal belongings are protected and can only be inspected with concrete evidence of a crime.</p><h2>Phrase three: ask for identification</h2><p><em>"Could you kindly give me your name and badge number, and confirm that you are authorized to carry out this procedure?"</em> Every authority is required to identify itself. That data is what later allows you to file a complaint with Internal Affairs or a Human Rights Commission.</p><h2>What to do if they still search you</h2><p>Do not struggle, run or insult. Recording in public places is legal. Tell someone you trust where you are. If they put you in a patrol car, keep quiet, do not sign anything you have not read and demand to contact a lawyer or the public defender.</p><p>Tone matters more than words: these phrases work because they are said calmly. It is about making the authority understand that you know your rights and that the interaction is being observed. This information is general and does not replace the advice of a lawyer.</p><p><small><strong>Editorial note:</strong> The basis of constitutional Article 16 is verifiable in official sources; the wording of the phrases and the advice come from information shared by Badabun and are advisory. Source: <a href="https://badabun.com/mexico/3-frases-legales-que-debes-decirle-a-un-policia-si-te-quiere-revisar/">Badabun</a></small></p>`,
        date: '2026-07-27',
        readTimeEs: '5 min de lectura',
