        contentType: 'news',
        slug: 'evo-morales-ordenes-captura',
        titleEs: 'Dos Ã³rdenes de captura y ninguna detenciÃ³n: Â¿por quÃ© Evo Morales sigue libre?',
        titleEn: 'Two arrest warrants and no detention: why is Evo Morales still free?',
        excerptEs: 'El expresidente acumula procesos por trata agravada y por terrorismo y alzamiento armado. La PolicÃ­a evita un operativo para prevenir choques con sus seguidores en el TrÃ³pico de Cochabamba.',
        excerptEn: 'The former president faces cases of aggravated human trafficking and terrorism and armed uprising. Police avoid an operation to prevent clashes with his followers in the Cochabamba tropics.',
        contentEs: `<h1>Dos Ã³rdenes de captura y ninguna detenciÃ³n: Â¿por quÃ© Evo Morales sigue libre?</h1><p><em>El expresidente boliviano acumula dos Ã³rdenes de captura sin ejecutar por trata agravada y por terrorismo. Resguardado por seguidores en el TrÃ³pico de Cochabamba, la PolicÃ­a evita un operativo para prevenir choques.</em></p><p>Evo Morales acumula dos Ã³rdenes de captura sin ejecutar: una por trata agravada de personas y otra por terrorismo y alzamiento armado. Los casos no avanzan porque la Justicia no ha podido detenerle, en medio de un escenario en el que el Gobierno ordenÃ³ priorizar su captura.</p><h2>El caso por trata agravada</h2><p>La FiscalÃ­a de Tarija iniciÃ³ la investigaciÃ³n en septiembre de 2024. La causa se refiere a la presunta relaciÃ³n de Morales con una menor en 2016, con quien, segÃºn la acusaciÃ³n, habrÃ­a tenido una hija cuando ejercÃ­a la Presidencia. Tras la acusaciÃ³n formal de octubre de 2025, un tribunal le declarÃ³ en rebeldÃ­a en mayo por no comparecer al juicio y dispuso una nueva orden de aprehensiÃ³n, que tampoco se ha ejecutado.</p><h2>La acusaciÃ³n por terrorismo</h2><p>La segunda orden deriva de los conflictos de mayo y junio, que dejaron 16 fallecidos â€”incluidos 13 que no pudieron recibir atenciÃ³n mÃ©dica por los bloqueos de vÃ­asâ€” y pÃ©rdidas de 3.000 millones de dÃ³lares. La denuncia partiÃ³ de lÃ­deres cÃ­vicos de Santa Cruz.</p><p>Morales niega su participaciÃ³n en las protestas, aunque arengÃ³ a mantener los bloqueos en discursos y redes sociales. El 24 de mayo escribiÃ³ en X que el presidente tenÃ­a "dos opciones" ante las protestas: "militarizar el paÃ­s" o llamar a elecciones en los prÃ³ximos 90 dÃ­as.</p><h2>La captura bloqueada por los apoyos</h2><p>La PolicÃ­a no ha ejecutado las Ã³rdenes porque sus seguidores controlan los accesos al TrÃ³pico de Cochabamba, donde Morales permanece desde finales de 2024. Las autoridades consideran que un operativo podrÃ­a provocar un enfrentamiento. En los Ãºltimos dos aÃ±os, los agentes se retiraron en al menos dos ocasiones ante las amenazas.</p><p>Un video de Morales conversando con dos policÃ­as en un puesto de control provocÃ³ un proceso disciplinario contra los agentes por "incumplir" resoluciones policiales.</p><h2>Dos visiones dentro de la PolicÃ­a</h2><p>El comandante de la PolicÃ­a, Mirko Sokol, afirmÃ³ que la captura "no es una prioridad" porque la instituciÃ³n mantiene unas 18.000 Ã³rdenes de aprehensiÃ³n pendientes. Sus declaraciones provocaron cuestionamientos de dirigentes oficialistas y de sectores opositores. Por su parte, el ministro de Gobierno, Marco Antonio Oviedo, sostuvo que se trata de una "tarea prioritaria".</p><p>El Gobierno de Rodrigo Paz ordenÃ³ a inicios de agosto priorizar la captura del expresidente: "Morales va a estar ante la justicia porque tiene que responder", asegurÃ³ el presidente.</p><p>Desde el TrÃ³pico, Morales sostiene que el proceso responde a una persecuciÃ³n polÃ­tica, afirmÃ³ que "va a demostrar" que es inocente de los delitos que se le atribuyen, negÃ³ haber impulsado acciones para forzar la salida de Paz y reiterÃ³ que no abandonarÃ¡ la regiÃ³n donde permanece desde hace casi dos aÃ±os.</p><p><small>Con informaciÃ³n de: <a href="https://efe.com/mundo/2026-08-09/ordenes-captura-evo-morales-por-que-sigue-libre/">EFE</a> y <a href="https://www.bolivia.com/actualidad/politica/evo-morales-enfrenta-procesos-por-trata-y-terrorismo-pero-la-policia-no-logra-ejecutar-su-captura-595774">Bolivia.com</a></small></p>`,
        contentEn: `<h1>Two arrest warrants and no detention: why is Evo Morales still free?</h1><p><em>The former Bolivian president has two unexecuted arrest warrants for aggravated trafficking and terrorism. Protected by followers in the Cochabamba tropics, police avoid an operation to prevent clashes.</em></p><p>Evo Morales has two unexecuted arrest warrants: one for aggravated human trafficking and another for terrorism and armed uprising. The cases have not advanced because justice has not been able to arrest him, in a context where the government has ordered his capture to be prioritized.</p><h2>The aggravated trafficking case</h2><p>Tarija's prosecutor's office began the investigation in September 2024. The case concerns Morales's alleged relationship with a minor in 2016, with whom, according to the accusation, he allegedly had a daughter while president. After the formal accusation in October 2025, a court declared him in contempt in May for failing to appear at trial and issued a new arrest order, which has also not been executed.</p><h2>The terrorism accusation</h2><p>The second order stems from the May-June conflicts, which left 16 dead â€” including 13 who could not receive medical care because of road blockades â€” and losses of $3 billion. The complaint came from civic leaders in Santa Cruz.</p><p>Morales denies involvement in the protests, although he urged people to maintain blockades in speeches and on social media. On May 24, he wrote on X that the president had "two options" in the face of the protests: "militarize the country" or call elections within 90 days.</p><h2>Capture blocked by supporters</h2><p>Police have not executed the orders because his followers control access to the Cochabamba tropics, where Morales has remained since late 2024. Authorities consider an operation could trigger a clash. In the past two years, officers have withdrawn at least twice in the face of threats.</p><p>A video of Morales talking to two police officers at a checkpoint triggered disciplinary proceedings against the officers for "failing to comply" with police orders.</p><h2>Two views within the police</h2><p>National Police commander Mirko Sokol said the capture is "not a priority" because the institution has some 18,000 pending arrest orders. His remarks drew criticism from both government-aligned leaders and opposition sectors. Meanwhile, Government Minister Marco Antonio Oviedo called it a "priority task".</p><p>Rodrigo Paz's government ordered at the start of August that the former president's capture be prioritized: "Morales will face justice because he has to answer," the president said.</p><p>From the tropics, Morales says the case is political persecution, claims he "will prove" his innocence of the crimes attributed to him, denies pushing actions to force Paz out, and reiterates he will not leave the region where he has remained for almost two years.</p><p><small>Source: <a href="https://efe.com/mundo/2026-08-09/ordenes-captura-evo-morales-por-que-sigue-libre/">EFE</a> and <a href="https://www.bolivia.com/actualidad/politica/evo-morales-enfrenta-procesos-por-trata-y-terrorismo-pero-la-policia-no-logra-ejecutar-su-captura-595774">Bolivia.com</a></small></p>`,
        date: '2026-08-09',
        readTimeEs: '6 min de lectura',
        readTimeEn: '5 min read',
        imageUrl: '/images/blog/bolivia-agosto-2026/evo-morales-ordenes-captura.webp',
        imageCaption: 'El expresidente Evo Morales en una apariciÃ³n anterior. Imagen: EFE / El Deber',
        categoryEs: 'PaÃ­s',
        categoryEn: 'Country',
        enableComments: true,
        featured: true,
        breaking: false,
        views: 1,
        location: 'TrÃ³pico de Cochabamba, Bolivia',
        source: 'EFE',
        region: 'Bolivia',
        sourceUrl: 'https://efe.com/mundo/2026-08-09/ordenes-captura-evo-morales-por-que-sigue-libre/'
