        contentEs: `<h1>Ã‰bola en RepÃºblica DemocrÃ¡tica del Congo: el brote supera los 5.000 casos confirmados</h1>
<p><em>El brote de Ã©bola por virus Bundibugyo en RepÃºblica DemocrÃ¡tica del Congo (RDC) superÃ³ los 5.000 casos confirmados: la OMS y el ECDC reportaron 5.021 casos y 2.378 muertes en el paÃ­s (tasa de letalidad del 47,4%) a fecha 16 de agosto, mÃ¡s 20 casos y 2 muertes en Uganda.</em></p>
<p>La OMS advirtiÃ³ que el brote, declarado emergencia de salud pÃºblica de importancia internacional el 17 de mayo, estÃ¡ en camino de convertirse en la epidemia de Ã©bola mÃ¡s letal jamÃ¡s registrada. La provincia de Ituri concentra la mayor carga â€”4.257 casos y 1.878 muertesâ€”, seguida de Kivu del Norte, Haut-Uele y Kivu del Sur, en el este del paÃ­s, una zona remota y golpeada por el conflicto.</p>
<p>Los trabajadores de salud pagan un alto precio: mÃ¡s de un centenar se han infectado y al menos 35 han fallecido. La falta de una vacuna autorizada contra la cepa Bundibugyo y el rastreo de contactos incompleto complican la respuesta. En Estados Unidos no se ha confirmado ningÃºn caso y el CDC considera bajo el riesgo para el pÃºblico.</p>
<p>Uganda declarÃ³ el fin de su brote el 28 de julio; todos sus casos fueron diagnosticados en Kampala, sin propagaciÃ³n comunitaria. Los paÃ­ses vecinos mantienen la vigilancia en la frontera.</p>
<p><small>Con informaciÃ³n de: <a href="https://www.who.int/emergencies/situations/ebola-outbreak---drc-2026">OMS</a>, <a href="https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/en/ebola-outbreak-democratic-republic-congo-and-uganda">ECDC</a> y <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/ebola/situation-summary/index.html">CDC</a>.</small></p>`,
        contentEn: `<h1>Ebola in DR Congo: outbreak surpasses 5,000 confirmed cases</h1>
<p><em>The Bundibugyo virus Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) has surpassed 5,000 confirmed cases: WHO and ECDC reported 5,021 cases and 2,378 deaths in the country (a 47.4% case fatality rate) as of August 16, plus 20 cases and 2 deaths in Uganda.</em></p>
<p>WHO warned the outbreak, declared a Public Health Emergency of International Concern on May 17, is on track to become the deadliest Ebola epidemic ever recorded. Ituri province carries the heaviest burden â€”4,257 cases and 1,878 deathsâ€” followed by North Kivu, Haut-Uele and South Kivu, in the remote, conflict-hit east of the country.</p>
<p>Health workers are paying a high price: more than a hundred have been infected and at least 35 have died. The lack of a licensed vaccine for the Bundibugyo strain and incomplete contact tracing complicate the response. No cases have been confirmed in the United States, and the CDC considers the risk to the public low.</p>
<p>Uganda declared its outbreak over on July 28; all its cases were diagnosed in Kampala, with no community spread. Neighboring countries are maintaining border surveillance.</p>
<p><small>Sources: <a href="https://www.who.int/emergencies/situations/ebola-outbreak---drc-2026">WHO</a>, <a href="https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/en/ebola-outbreak-democratic-republic-congo-and-uganda">ECDC</a> and <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/ebola/situation-summary/index.html">CDC</a>.</small></p>`,
