        contentEs: `<h1>La economÃ­a mundial 2026 a la sombra de la guerra</h1>
<p><em>El Fondo Monetario Internacional titulÃ³ su informe de abril de 2026 "La economÃ­a mundial a la sombra de la guerra". El mensaje es claro: la geopolÃ­tica vuelve a ser el principal riesgo macroeconÃ³mico.</em></p>

<figure class="report-figure"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1469474968028-56623f02e42e?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop" alt="EconomÃ­a global" loading="lazy"><figcaption>La incertidumbre geopolÃ­tica pesa sobre el crecimiento global. Foto: Unsplash</figcaption></figure>

<h2 class="report-chapter">Crecimiento y precios</h2>
<p>El FMI proyecta que el crecimiento global se desacelere al 3,1% en 2026 y al 3,2% en 2027, por debajo de los promedios previos a la pandemia. La inflaciÃ³n global repuntarÃ¡ levemente al 4,4% en 2026 antes de retomar su caÃ­da. Los riesgos estÃ¡n decididamente a la baja: un conflicto mÃ¡s largo o amplio, una fragmentaciÃ³n geopolÃ­tica mayor, o una desilusiÃ³n sobre la productividad de la IA podrÃ­an debilitar el crecimiento y sacudir los mercados.</p>
<div class="stat-grid"><div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">3,1%</div><div class="stat-label">Crecimiento global 2026 (FMI)</div></div><div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">4,4%</div><div class="stat-label">InflaciÃ³n global 2026</div></div><div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">$58</div><div class="stat-label">Brent promedio 2026 (EIA)</div></div><div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">2,5%</div><div class="stat-label">Escenario adverso de crecimiento</div></div>

<h2 class="report-chapter">PetrÃ³leo y fragmentaciÃ³n</h2>
<p>La EIA de EE. UU. prevÃ© un Brent promedio de 58 dÃ³lares en 2026, presionado por el exceso de oferta de la OPEP+ y la debilidad de la demanda. Pero los choques energÃ©ticos â€”como el cierre de Ormuzâ€” pueden revertir rÃ¡pidamente esa tendencia. La fragmentaciÃ³n del comercio, con aranceles rÃ©cord entre EE. UU. y China, aÃ±ade costos y fricciones a las cadenas globales.</p>
<a class="doc-card" href="https://www.imf.org/en/publications/weo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><div class="doc-card-icon blue">FMI</div><div><div class="doc-card-title">World Economic Outlook, abril 2026</div><div class="doc-card-desc">"La economÃ­a mundial a la sombra de la guerra".</div></div></a>
<div class="expert-quote"><p class="expert-quote-text">&ldquo;Los riesgos a la baja dominan el panorama. Un conflicto mÃ¡s prolongado, una fragmentaciÃ³n mayor o una decepciÃ³n sobre la IA pueden debilitar seriamente el crecimiento.&rdquo;</p><div class="expert-quote-author"><div class="expert-quote-initial">FMI</div><div><div class="expert-quote-name">Fondo Monetario Internacional</div><div class="expert-quote-role">WEO abril 2026</div></div></div></div>
<p>En un escenario severo, con dislocaciones energÃ©ticas persistentes, la economÃ­a global rozarÃ­a la recesiÃ³n, con inflaciÃ³n cercana al 6%. La deuda pÃºblica elevada y los buffers de polÃ­tica erosionados amplifican la vulnerabilidad.</p>
<p><small>Con informaciÃ³n de: FMI (WEO abril 2026), U.S. EIA y JP Morgan Global Research.</small></p>`,
        contentEn: `<h1>The world economy 2026 in the shadow of war</h1>
<p><em>The IMF titled its April 2026 report "The world economy in the shadow of war". The message is clear: geopolitics is again the main macroeconomic risk.</em></p>

<figure class="report-figure"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1469474968028-56623f02e42e?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop" alt="Global economy" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Geopolitical uncertainty weighs on global growth. Photo: Unsplash</figcaption></figure>

<h2 class="report-chapter">Growth and prices</h2>
<p>The IMF projects global growth to slow to 3.1% in 2026 and 3.2% in 2027, below pre-pandemic averages. Global inflation will tick up to 4.4% in 2026 before resuming its decline. Downside risks dominate: a longer or broader conflict, deeper geopolitical fragmentation, or disappointment over AI productivity could weaken growth and unsettle markets.</p>
<div class="stat-grid"><div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">3.1%</div><div class="stat-label">Global growth 2026 (IMF)</div></div><div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">4.4%</div><div class="stat-label">Global inflation 2026</div></div><div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">$58</div><div class="stat-label">Average Brent 2026 (EIA)</div></div><div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">2.5%</div><div class="stat-label">Adverse scenario growth</div></div></div>

<h2 class="report-chapter">Oil and fragmentation</h2>
<p>The U.S. EIA forecasts an average Brent of $58 in 2026, pressured by OPEC+ supply surplus and weak demand. But energy shocks â€”like the Hormuz closureâ€” can quickly reverse that trend. Trade fragmentation, with record tariffs between the U.S. and China, adds costs and frictions to global chains.</p>
<a class="doc-card" href="https://www.imf.org/en/publications/weo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><div class="doc-card-icon blue">IMF</div><div><div class="doc-card-title">World Economic Outlook, April 2026</div><div class="doc-card-desc">"The world economy in the shadow of war".</div></div></a>
<div class="expert-quote"><p class="expert-quote-text">&ldquo;Downside risks dominate the outlook. A longer conflict, greater fragmentation or an AI disappointment can seriously weaken growth.&rdquo;</p><div class="expert-quote-author"><div class="expert-quote-initial">IMF</div><div><div class="expert-quote-name">International Monetary Fund</div><div class="expert-quote-role">WEO April 2026</div></div></div></div>
<p>In a severe scenario, with persistent energy dislocations, the global economy would approach recession, with inflation near 6%. High public debt and eroded policy buffers amplify vulnerability.</p>
<p><small>With information from: IMF (WEO April 2026), U.S. EIA and JP Morgan Global Research.</small></p>`,
